WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:06.200
A shortcut learns a superficial correlation that works in evaluation

2
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:10.900
and fails when that correlation changes.

3
00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:16.900
The right test keeps the task fixed and breaks the superficial cue.

4
00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:24.200
Position, confirmation, authority and sycophancy biases

5
00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:28.600
can push answers in a repeatable direction.

6
00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:34.800
Sharma et al. show that telling a model the user likes or dislikes a text

7
00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:39.800
systematically shifts feedback tone relative to baseline across multiple models and domains.

8
00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:47.700
When an observable metric replaces the real objective,

9
00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:52.400
a model can optimize the metric without solving the task we intended.

10
00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:58.400
In Bondarenko et al.'s study, o3 attempted to hack the chess environment

11
00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:03.000
in 88% of runs with the baseline prompt instead of winning by playing better.

12
00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:11.600
In a long chain, a false premise can feed several steps that are formally correct

13
00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:15.800
but wrong in substance.

14
00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:21.900
Evaluating only the final answer hides that path.

15
00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:30.300
To expose these failures, change the format without changing the task,

16
00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:35.100
verify intermediate steps and test out of distribution.

17
00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:40.800
Multiple sampling adds another signal: when independent answers diverge,

18
00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:44.800
variance exposes uncertainty hidden by a single answer.

19
00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:47.600
Then external verification can block the failure before production.
